B.NM=new A.ac(B.ak,["Assets = Liabilities + Equity. Profit = Revenue \u2212 Expenses. Cash differs from profit because of timing, working capital and non-cash items.","Explain one fictional month-end variance in each statement. For each, identify the source record, reconciliation and control you would test.","Prepare a one-page month-end health note with profit, financial position, cash movements, reconciliations, risks and action owners.","Performance over a period, financial position at a date and cash movement answer different questions. Tie each to evidence."],t.w)
B.NP=new A.ac(B.ak,["Working capital = Current assets \u2212 Current liabilities. CCC = DIO + DSO \u2212 DPO. Use consistent period and denominator definitions.","Build a weekly cash-risk view with opening cash, expected receipts, critical payments, confidence, owner and contingency.","Recommend a 30-day working-capital plan with baseline, segmented actions, owners, expected impact and supplier/customer risk guardrails.","Liquidity depends on timing. Improve collections, inventory and payment planning together without breaching terms."],t.w)
B.O5=new A.ac(B.ak,["Reconciliation difference = Source A \u2212 Source B. A zero difference is not enough if both sources are incomplete or share the same error.","Design a reconciliation control: purpose, sources, frequency, preparer, reviewer, tolerance, evidence, escalation and closure rules.","Create a supplier-payment control matrix listing risks, preventive/detective controls, owners, evidence and failure response.","Controls need a risk objective, owner, evidence, timely execution, independent review and a defined response to failure."],t.w)
B.LA=s([B.NM,B.NP,B.O5],t.SN)
B.O_=new A.ac(B.ak,["First-time match rate = invoices matched without correction \xf7 invoices processed \xd7 100%. Overdue rate = overdue unpaid invoices \xf7 total unpaid invoices \xd7 100%.","Create an exception log with supplier, invoice, amount, PO, receipt, reason, owner, age, next action, due date and escalation flag.","Propose a 30-day overdue-invoice reduction plan with root causes, weekly targets and control guardrails.","P2P speed comes from clean data, reliable matching and clear exception ownership\u2014not bypassing approvals."],t.w)
B.O6=new A.ac(B.ak,["DSO \u2248 average trade receivables \xf7 credit sales for period \xd7 days in period. Track dispute age, promise-to-pay kept rate and unapplied cash.","Prioritise five fictional overdue invoices by value, age and risk; specify action, owner, promise date and escalation.","Design an O2C sprint to reduce aged debt and unapplied cash without harming customer relationships or control quality.","Overdue is a symptom. Separate billing, delivery, credit, dispute and application issues so the right owner can resolve them."],t.w)
B.O1=new A.ac(B.ak,["Close completion = tasks completed and approved \xf7 tasks due \xd7 100%. Pair speed with post-close adjustments and aged-reconciliation metrics.","Draft a five-day close calendar with task, owner, dependency, evidence, reviewer and escalation.","Design a close dashboard tracking completion, late journals, aged reconciling items, unsupported balances and post-close corrections.","A green close status is not proof of accuracy; use cut-off, supported entries, reconciliations and review."],t.w)
B.Lk=s([B.O_,B.O6,B.O1],t.SN)
B.NT=new A.ac(B.ak,['Example: =IF(AND([@Status]="Unpaid",[@DueDate]<$B$1),"Overdue","Not overdue"). Use SUMIFS for owner-level overdue value.',"Define a report date, overdue rule, ageing bands and duplicate key. List three tests to validate the output.","Specify an AP ageing report with formulas, KPI definitions, quality checks, owner breakdown and action list.","Define the grain and rule, clean inputs, calculate transparently, reconcile outputs and explain the action."],t.w)
B.NU=new A.ac(B.ak,["Extract \u2192 Transform \u2192 Validate \u2192 Load. Eligible source rows should equal loaded rows plus explicitly rejected/held rows after documented filters.","Document schema, type fixes, ID mapping, duplicate rules, exception handling and reconciliation checks for two monthly files.","Design a refreshable AP pipeline with source convention, mapping owner, error query, reconciliation and failure notification.","Repeatability matters only when transformations are explainable and every refresh reconciles to source."],t.w)
B.O2=new A.ac(B.ak,["Overdue rate = overdue unpaid invoice count \xf7 total unpaid invoice count \xd7 100%. Keep count-based and value-based rates distinct.","Create a KPI dictionary for overdue value, first-time match rate, blocked count and average resolution age.","Wireframe an AP dashboard with trend, breakdown, drill-through, source reconciliation and refresh/error indicators.","Reliable BI requires clear KPI definitions, correct model grain, tested measures and governed refresh."],t.w)
B.K7=s([B.NT,B.NU,B.O2],t.SN)
B.O4=new A.ac(B.ak,["Net annual benefit = measurable annual benefit \u2212 annual run/support cost. Payback months = initial investment \xf7 monthly net benefit, if positive.","Score invoice entry, dispute resolution and monthly file consolidation for volume, rule stability, exceptions, risk and value.","Prepare an automation brief with baseline, solution choice, controls, test cases, fallback, owner and monitoring.","Automate stable work, not uncontrolled complexity; measure real benefits and include support and exception costs."],t.w)
B.NY=new A.ac(B.ak,["Field accuracy = correctly extracted and validated fields \xf7 fields tested \xd7 100%. Track false accepts separately from false rejects.","Define validation and hard-stop rules for supplier, invoice number, date, currency, tax, total and PO. Assign each exception an owner.","Design invoice processing with confidence bands, validations, human queue, audit fields, sampling and rollback.","Extraction proposes data; business validation checks it; authorised people resolve uncertainty."],t.w)
B.NS=new A.ac(B.ak,["Precision = true positive alerts \xf7 all positive alerts. Recall = true positive alerts \xf7 all actual positive cases.","Write an AI control policy covering allowed use, reviewer authority, evidence, privacy, escalation and monitoring.","Create a governance pack with risk assessment, human review workflow, test results, logs, KPIs and shutdown criteria.","AI can prioritise and assist, but accountable people verify evidence, record overrides and monitor performance."],t.w)
B.Lh=s([B.O4,B.NY,B.NS],t.SN)
B.NZ=new A.ac(B.ak,["Reconciliation break = internal recorded amount \u2212 externally confirmed amount. Compare counts and IDs as well as totals.","Draw the payment lifecycle and list evidence for pending, rejected, timeout and confirmed settlement.","Specify payment reconciliation with IDs, status mapping, settlement windows, duplicate protection, exception queue and ownership.","An API response is not settlement proof. Trace state and reconcile independent records before retrying."],t.w)
B.NL=new A.ac(B.ak,["Source completeness: eligible transactions = successfully processed + explicitly rejected/held items after documented exclusions. Reconcile count and value.","Write a timeout-handling flow including ID, status inquiry, idempotency, bounded retry, exception route and audit log.","Design ERP-to-provider integration controls for API contract, access, retries, monitoring, reconciliation and incidents.","Design for timeouts and retries; use idempotency to avoid duplicate effects and reconcile transaction outcomes."],t.w)
B.NQ=new A.ac(B.ak,["A simple risk-priority score may be likelihood \xd7 impact, but treat it as a ranking aid unless supported by validated quantitative evidence.","Create a bank-detail-change checklist covering trusted-channel verification, maker-checker, access, evidence and escalation.","Run a tabletop exercise for payment redirection: detection, containment, evidence, stakeholders, recovery and lessons learned.","Independently verify sensitive changes, restrict access, preserve evidence and escalate suspicious activity."],t.w)
B.LF=s([B.NZ,B.NL,B.NQ],t.SN)
B.NO=new A.ac(B.ak,["Improvement % = (baseline \u2212 new result) \xf7 baseline \xd7 100% for lower-is-better measures, where baseline is non-zero.","Map a blocked-invoice process, identify five handoffs and three possible root causes, and specify evidence needed to validate them.","Create a problem statement, current-state map, root-cause summary and prioritised improvement backlog.","Measure first, map real work and validate root causes before selecting a solution."],t.w)
B.NR=new A.ac(B.ak,["Story format: As a [role], I want [capability], so that [business value]. Criteria must be specific, observable and testable.","Write a user story and five pass/fail criteria for reducing blocked invoices, including reconciliation, exception visibility and access control.","Prepare a backlog item with problem, story, criteria, dependencies, risks, tests and Definition of Done.","A story explains who needs what and why; acceptance criteria prove the outcome; Definition of Done protects quality."],t.w)
B.O0=new A.ac(B.ak,["Benefit realisation % = validated benefit achieved \xf7 approved target benefit \xd7 100%, using comparable measures.","Create a 30/60/90-day plan with milestone, owner, dependency, risk, training action, KPI and evidence; separate outputs from outcomes.","Prepare an executive recommendation with baseline, root causes, phased roadmap, cost/benefit assumptions, controls, adoption and benefit reviews.","Delivery is not benefit realisation. Track outcomes, adoption and control guardrails after launch."],t.w)
B.JQ=s([B.NO,B.NR,B.O0],t.SN)
B.NN=new A.ac(B.ak,["Use Excel Tables and structured references. SUMIFS sums by multiple criteria; COUNTIFS counts matches; IF/AND can flag overdue unpaid invoices. Fix a report date for repeatable results.","Build an invoice ageing table with validated dates, payment status, duplicate checks and four ageing buckets.","Create a reconciled AP ageing pack with an exceptions log, KPI definitions, refresh instructions and reviewer checks.","A formula is only trustworthy when inputs, definitions, edge cases and source totals are controlled."],t.w)
B.O3=new A.ac(B.ak,["IF(condition, result_if_true, result_if_false); IFERROR should handle expected cases deliberately, not hide every data-quality problem. Use absolute references for fixed assumptions.","Write and test formulas for overdue status, missing due date, duplicate invoice ID, and amount variance. Include tests for blank and invalid values.","Create a formula test sheet showing expected outputs for normal, boundary, missing and invalid data.","Choose formulas for explicit business rules and test both normal and exception cases."],t.w)
B.NW=new A.ac(B.ak,["XLOOKUP(lookup_value, lookup_array, return_array, if_not_found, match_mode). Define exact-match behaviour and check key uniqueness before lookup.","Match invoices to a supplier master and identify missing suppliers, duplicate keys and mismatched payment terms.","Build a supplier validation worksheet with lookup results, exceptions and resolution ownership.","A lookup does not prove the data is correct; key quality and unmatched records must be checked."],t.w)
B.NX=new A.ac(B.ak,["A reliable query should document source, steps, data types, rejected rows, row counts and control totals. Merge joins on keys; append stacks compatible tables.","Combine two monthly files, standardise supplier names and dates, and reconcile input versus output record counts and totals.","Create a refreshable monthly pipeline with a documented exception table and a refresh checklist.","Repeatability is valuable only when refresh results are validated."],t.w)
B.NV=new A.ac(B.ak,["Ageing bucket totals should equal the reconciled open balance under the stated inclusion rules. KPI = defined numerator \xf7 defined denominator; document exclusions.","Produce a dashboard with open balance, overdue balance, ageing buckets, top exceptions and action owners.","Present a monthly AP risk pack with data dictionary, reconciliations, controls, KPI definitions and management actions.","A useful dashboard is reconciled, defined, explainable and linked to accountable action."],t.w)
B.KU=s([B.NN,B.O3,B.NW,B.NX,B.NV],t.SN)
B.vl=new A.ac(B.Pg,[B.LA,B.Lk,B.K7,B.Lh,B.LF,B.JQ,B.KU],A.ao("ac<A,Y<aO<A,A>>>"))
B.P8={CORE_3:0,CORE_4:1,OPS_3:2,OPS_4:3,DATA_3:4,DATA_4:5,AI_3:6,AI_4:7,TECH_3:8,TECH_4:9,CHANGE_3:10,CHANGE_4:11,EXCEL_0:12,EXCEL_1:13,EXCEL_2:14,EXCEL_3:15,EXCEL_4:16}
B.aT={focus:0,concept:1,example:2,steps:3,cross:4,formula:5,assignment:6,capstone:7,gist:8}
B.Od=new A.ac(B.aT,["Applied financial statement analysis: analyse trends, margins, liquidity, leverage and cash conversion together. A single ratio rarely explains performance. Compare periods, investigate material movements, and note accounting-policy or one-off effects before drawing conclusions.","A practical analysis starts with reliable, comparable statements. Horizontal analysis compares a line item across periods; vertical analysis expresses items as a percentage of a base such as revenue or total assets. Ratios turn balances into interpretable relationships: gross margin, operating margin, current ratio, debt-to-equity and operating cash flow to profit. Interpret each ratio in context, not as an isolated verdict. Confirm the reporting period, entity scope, currency, sign conventions, reclassifications and whether balances are averages or closing values.","A fictional company reports revenue rising from \u20b910 crore to \u20b912 crore, while operating profit rises only from \u20b91.2 crore to \u20b91.26 crore. Revenue growth is 20%, but operating margin falls from 12% to 10.5%. The analyst should investigate product mix, discounts, input costs, payroll, logistics and one-off expenses. If receivables also rise faster than revenue and operating cash flow weakens, growth may be absorbing cash. State what the numbers show and what additional evidence is required before attributing a cause.","1. Obtain approved statements and trial-balance mapping for at least two comparable periods.\n2. Check completeness, currency, period length, sign conventions and material reclassifications.\n3. Calculate absolute and percentage movements; flag material or unexpected variances.\n4. Calculate relevant margins, liquidity, leverage and cash-conversion measures.\n5. Trace each significant movement to ledger details, operational drivers and supporting evidence.\n6. Separate confirmed facts from hypotheses; document limitations and owner actions.\n7. Present a concise conclusion with risk, cash implications and follow-up tests.","Finance validates accounting mappings and explanations; FP&A provides budget and forecast context; Sales explains volume, pricing and customer mix; Operations explains capacity, yield and input costs; Treasury reviews liquidity and debt; Audit/Risk challenges evidence and control implications.","Revenue growth = (current revenue \u2212 prior revenue) \xf7 prior revenue. Operating margin = operating profit \xf7 revenue. Current ratio = current assets \xf7 current liabilities. Calculate only when denominators are meaningful and compare consistent periods.","Use a fictional two-year statement with at least six line items. Calculate absolute change, percentage change, gross margin, operating margin and current ratio. Select three unusual movements and write the evidence you would request before explaining each.","Produce a two-page management analysis containing a trend table, five ratios, a profit-to-cash bridge, three evidence-backed findings, two unresolved hypotheses, liquidity risks and action owners. Include formulas and a clear reporting date.","Analyse trends and ratios together, reconcile the data first, investigate drivers with evidence, and label assumptions honestly."],t.w)
B.Oh=new A.ac(B.aT,["Working-capital improvement workshop: diagnose the full cash-conversion cycle without shifting risk unfairly to suppliers or customers. Prioritise root causes such as inaccurate demand, billing delays, disputes, poor master data and unclear ownership.","Working capital is operational cash tied up in current assets net of current liabilities. Sustainable improvement is usually achieved by preventing avoidable inventory, invoicing accurately and promptly, resolving disputes, applying cash correctly and paying according to agreed terms. DIO, DSO and DPO depend on consistent cost-of-sales, revenue and purchases bases. A lower cycle is not automatically better if it results from stock-outs, aggressive collections, missed discounts or supplier distress.","A distributor has annual credit sales of \u20b936.5 crore and average receivables of \u20b96 crore, implying DSO of about 60 days using 365 days. If clean billing, proof-of-delivery capture and dispute ownership reduce average receivables to \u20b95 crore, the approximate cash release is \u20b91 crore. Validate that the reduction is not caused by factoring, write-offs or a seasonal comparison. Track dispute recurrence and customer service alongside DSO.","1. Set a dated baseline for DIO, DSO, DPO and the cash conversion cycle.\n2. Segment balances by value, ageing, entity, customer/supplier and root-cause code.\n3. Sample high-value items and verify dates, terms, documents and system status.\n4. Distinguish controllable process delays from commercial terms and external constraints.\n5. Assign actions: forecast and inventory parameters, billing accuracy, dispute resolution, cash application or supplier-term governance.\n6. Estimate cash release using transparent assumptions and avoid double counting.\n7. Monitor service, write-offs, supplier health and control failures after implementation.","Treasury validates cash forecasts; Sales and Credit manage customer terms; Billing and Logistics resolve proof and invoice issues; Procurement agrees supplier terms; Operations improves inventory planning; IT fixes data/workflow causes; Finance measures verified cash impact.","Cash conversion cycle = DIO + DSO \u2212 DPO. Approximate receivables cash release = reduction in average receivables, after checking seasonality, write-offs and financing effects.","Build an action register with five working-capital issues. For each, record baseline, root cause, evidence, owner, action, expected cash impact, service/control guardrail and review date.","Design a 60-day working-capital improvement plan with a reconciled baseline, quantified opportunity, initiatives, owners, dependencies, risks and weekly measures. Include at least one guardrail for customers and suppliers.","Improve the cause of cash delay, not just the KPI. Validate cash release and protect service, controls and commercial relationships."],t.w)
B.Oo=new A.ac(B.aT,["AP exception investigation: classify blocked invoices by reason, quantify value and age, trace evidence across PO, receipt and invoice records, and route each case to an accountable resolver. A queue is useful only when the next action is clear.","An AP exception process should distinguish price variance, quantity variance, missing receipt, duplicate risk, tax or master-data mismatch, missing approval and interface failure. Use a unique invoice key and preserve the source document. Ageing alone is not root cause. Prioritisation should consider value, due date, supplier criticality, risk and time waiting for another team. A blocked item must not be forced through or split to bypass controls.","A \u20b94.8 lakh invoice is blocked because the invoice quantity is 120 units while the approved PO and receipt support 100. AP confirms the source invoice is legible, then routes the discrepancy to Receiving and the buyer. The buyer verifies whether a receipt is missing or the invoice is incorrect. The case log records evidence, owner, next action and due date; payment is released only through the authorised resolution.","1. Preserve invoice ID, supplier, amount, currency, PO, receipt, due date and source image.\n2. Check duplicate indicators and validate supplier/bank details using approved sources.\n3. Assign a controlled exception reason; do not use a generic \u201cother\u201d without explanation.\n4. Compare invoice, PO, receipt, tax and approval evidence.\n5. Route to the role able to resolve the cause and set an SLA.\n6. Record decision, approver, evidence and any permitted adjustment.\n7. Trend repeat causes and fix upstream process or master-data issues.","AP owns triage and evidence; Procurement confirms commercial terms; Receiving validates goods/services; Tax checks statutory treatment; Master Data validates approved supplier changes; IT investigates interfaces; Treasury handles payment timing after release controls pass.","Exception rate = invoices with a defined exception \xf7 invoices received in the period. Also monitor exception value, median age, repeat-cause rate and percentage resolved within SLA; define denominator and period explicitly.","Create a case log for six fictional blocked invoices. Include unique key, reason, value, age, evidence missing, accountable owner, next action, SLA, control risk and final resolution status.","Prepare an AP exception dashboard specification and a worked case file. Reconcile total invoice count/value to source, show ageing and cause, and design an escalation rule that never bypasses approval controls.","Preserve evidence, identify the actual mismatch, assign a resolver and track the cause through a controlled resolution."],t.w)
B.Og=new A.ac(B.aT,["O2C collections and cash application: distinguish a truly overdue debt from billing disputes, missing remittances, unapplied receipts and credit notes. A good collection plan combines customer contact with accurate ledgers and timely dispute resolution.","Order-to-cash spans order validation, credit review, fulfilment, billing, accounts receivable, collection, cash application and dispute closure. A receipt can arrive in the bank while the invoice remains open if remittance details are missing or the payment is short. Ageing should use a stated date and contractual due date. Collection prioritisation can combine amount, age, risk, promise-to-pay status and dispute reason; contact must follow policy and customer communication standards.","A customer has three open invoices totalling \u20b97.2 lakh and remits \u20b95 lakh with an unclear reference. The bank statement proves receipt, but not the intended allocation. Cash application checks payer identity, remittance advice, invoice amounts and deductions. The \u20b95 lakh is posted to the correct invoices only after evidence supports the match; the remaining balance and any disputed deduction receive separate owners and follow-up dates.","1. Freeze a dated AR snapshot and reconcile subledger to GL.\n2. Match bank receipts using transaction references, payer and remittance evidence.\n3. Separate unapplied cash, disputed invoices, credit-note requests and genuine non-payment.\n4. Prioritise by exposure, ageing, risk, customer commitments and resolution blockers.\n5. Contact customers with invoice-level evidence and a clear requested action.\n6. Log promises, disputes, deductions, owners and next dates.\n7. Review repeat billing errors and measure collection outcomes and customer impact.","Collections owns contact strategy; Billing corrects invoice defects; Sales manages commercial context; Customer Service confirms delivery disputes; Cash Application matches receipts; Credit Risk governs exposure; Treasury and GL ensure bank and ledger reconciliation.","DSO \u2248 average trade receivables \xf7 credit sales for the period \xd7 days in the period. Define whether using closing or average balances, and do not treat DSO as a substitute for invoice-level ageing.","Create an AR action list for eight fictional customers, including overdue value, dispute/unapplied-cash flags, risk, promise date, next action and owner. Explain which balances should not receive a standard collection reminder yet.","Build an O2C weekly review pack with reconciled AR ageing, unapplied cash, dispute root causes, promise-to-pay performance, escalation rules and measurable actions.","Collections work best when receipt allocation and billing accuracy are fixed alongside customer follow-up."],t.w)
B.Oi=new A.ac(B.aT,["Excel formulas and ageing analysis practice lab: establish a consistent as-of date, classify open invoices into mutually exclusive ageing buckets, validate formula behaviour for blanks and credits, and reconcile both row counts and value.","A reliable ageing analysis starts with a defined dataset grain\u2014one row per invoice or open invoice line\u2014and fields for invoice ID, supplier/customer, invoice date, due date, open amount, status and report date. Use an explicit as-of date rather than TODAY() when reproducibility matters. IF and AND can classify status; SUMIFS aggregates value by criteria; COUNTIFS counts records; XLOOKUP or a controlled mapping table can enrich records. Formula outputs are not trusted until blanks, dates, duplicates, signs and totals are tested.","With report date 31 October, an unpaid invoice due 15 October is 16 days overdue; an invoice due 5 November is not overdue. A robust rule first checks whether the record is open and the due date is valid, then calculates days overdue as MAX(0, report date \u2212 due date). Bucket boundaries should be documented, for example current, 1\u201330, 31\u201360, 61\u201390 and over 90 days.","1. Convert the source range to a table and preserve the raw-data tab.\n2. Confirm unique invoice keys, date types, currency, status and open-amount sign.\n3. Put the as-of date in one controlled cell and reference it in formulas.\n4. Create overdue-days and ageing-bucket columns with explicit blank/status handling.\n5. Summarise amount and count with SUMIFS/COUNTIFS or a pivot table.\n6. Test boundary dates, credit balances, blank due dates and paid invoices.\n7. Reconcile totals and row counts to the source; document exclusions and refresh date.","AP/AR validates operational meaning; Data/BI reviews transformations; Finance confirms totals and cut-off; IT protects source access; Audit checks repeatability and evidence; managers use the segmented output to assign actions.",'Overdue days = MAX(0, as-of date \u2212 due date), only for open records with a valid due date. Example bucket: =IF(Status<>"Open","Closed",IF(DueDate="","Missing due date",IF(AsOfDate<=DueDate,"Current",IF(AsOfDate-DueDate<=30,"1-30",IF(AsOfDate-DueDate<=60,"31-60",IF(AsOfDate-DueDate<=90,"61-90","90+")))))). Adapt structured references to your table.',"Create a small ageing sheet with at least 15 fictional invoices, including paid, future-due, 1\u201330, 31\u201360, over-90, blank-date and credit-value examples. Show formulas and reconcile row count and amount to the source.","Deliver an Excel ageing workbook with raw data, controlled as-of date, validated formulas, summary pivot, exception list, reconciliation checks and a short management interpretation.","Define grain and report date, classify edge cases deliberately, and reconcile counts and amounts before using the ageing report."],t.w)
B.On=new A.ac(B.aT,["Power Query refresh and reconciliation workshop: build a repeatable transformation pipeline that preserves lineage, identifies unmapped rows and produces the same validated output when the source refreshes.","Power Query separates source extraction from repeatable transformation steps. Append combines compatible row sets; Merge joins tables using keys and join type. Data types, trimmed text, null handling and key uniqueness affect results. A left anti join can expose records without a mapping. Refresh success does not prove business correctness: row counts, distinct keys, unmatched rows and monetary totals should be reconciled before the output is consumed.","Three monthly AP files have different header casing and supplier IDs containing extra spaces. The query standardises column names and types, trims IDs, appends the months, then merges a supplier mapping table. Unmatched suppliers are retained in a separate exception output instead of being silently dropped. The analyst compares source row counts and total open value with the transformed dataset and explains any permitted exclusions.","1. Keep source files unchanged and document their location and expected schema.\n2. Import using a stable folder or workbook connector and standardise headers/types.\n3. Trim and clean key fields without changing legitimate identifiers.\n4. Append files with compatible schemas; use Merge only when a documented key exists.\n5. Produce a visible unmatched/missing-key exception query.\n6. Compare counts, distinct business keys and totals before and after transformation.\n7. Refresh with a new test file and confirm the process remains stable.","Finance defines the expected population and totals; Data Engineering maintains source contracts; Master Data owns mappings; IT controls access and refresh credentials; Audit reviews lineage and repeatability; report users confirm the result answers the intended question.","Row reconciliation: source eligible rows = output rows + documented exclusions, after accounting for any intentional aggregation. Value reconciliation: source eligible amount = output amount + documented differences. State the grain before comparing counts.","Design a query flow for three monthly files and a supplier mapping table. List each transformation, join key, expected row count, unmatched-row treatment and amount check.","Create a refresh-ready Power Query specification with source inventory, transformation steps, mapping exceptions, reconciliation results, refresh ownership and a failure/recovery procedure.","Repeatable transformations need preserved source data, controlled joins, visible exceptions and reconciled output."],t.w)
B.Oe=new A.ac(B.aT,["Finance automation business-case lab: compare the current process with a proposed future state, quantify benefits and full costs, assess control changes, and define a pilot with measurable acceptance criteria.","Automation selection should follow process stabilisation and root-cause analysis. Candidate approaches include workflow for routing and approvals, RPA for repetitive stable interfaces, APIs for system-to-system exchange, OCR/IDP for document extraction and AI for bounded classification or prioritisation. A business case includes build and integration, licences or infrastructure, exception handling, support, monitoring, change management and control assurance\u2014not just saved clicks. Benefits must avoid double counting released time as cash savings unless capacity is actually removed or redeployed with evidence.","A team processes 20,000 invoices per year and spends six minutes on a repetitive capture step. At an illustrative loaded cost of \u20b9500 per hour, gross annual time capacity is about 2,000 hours or \u20b910 lakh. If automation captures 70% of that time and annual support costs \u20b93 lakh, the simple net capacity value is \u20b94 lakh before implementation cost and risk adjustments. This is not automatically \u20b94 lakh cash saving; validate exception rates, actual redeployment and control effectiveness.","1. Baseline volume, touch time, rework, error rate, cycle time and control failures.\n2. Map variants and exceptions; stabilise process and master data first.\n3. Compare workflow, RPA, API, OCR/IDP and AI against suitability and risk.\n4. Estimate build, integration, support, monitoring and change costs.\n5. Calculate benefits with explicit assumptions and avoid double counting.\n6. Define a limited pilot, acceptance thresholds, human fallback and rollback.\n7. Compare pilot outcomes with baseline and obtain control-owner approval before scale.","Process owners validate workflow; Finance challenges benefits; IT/Architecture assesses supportability; Security reviews access and data; Risk/Audit evaluates control design; HR/change leads adoption; Operations owns exceptions and service continuity.","Net annual benefit = validated annual benefit \u2212 recurring annual costs. Simple payback period = one-time implementation cost \xf7 annual net benefit, only when net benefit is positive and timing assumptions are appropriate.","Prepare a one-page business case for automating a stable invoice-capture task. State baseline, proposed method, assumptions, one-time/recurring costs, quantified benefits, control risks and pilot pass/fail criteria.","Build a decision pack comparing two automation options, including cost model, process map, risk/control assessment, exception route, 90-day pilot and benefit verification plan.","Automate a stable, measured process; count full costs and exceptions; prove benefits and controls before scaling."],t.w)
B.Op=new A.ac(B.aT,["Invoice document validation workshop: separate extraction from validation, test critical fields against business evidence, route uncertain cases to a reviewer and preserve a traceable decision record.","OCR converts image content into text; intelligent document processing may classify documents and extract fields. Neither proves that a field is correct or that a transaction is legitimate. Validation should combine format checks, arithmetic, supplier status, duplicate indicators, PO/receipt match, currency, tax rules and approval policy. Confidence thresholds should be calibrated on representative documents and monitored by field and supplier segment. Reviewers need the source image, extracted values, reason for the flag and authority to correct or reject.","An invoice model extracts \u20b91,80,000 with high confidence, but line totals add to \u20b91,08,000 and the supplier bank account differs from the approved master. The amount must not be auto-approved because extraction confidence only describes the model\u2019s estimate. Route the arithmetic discrepancy and bank-detail change through separate controlled checks, verify the change using a previously trusted channel, and log the decision.","1. Capture the source file securely and associate a unique document ID.\n2. Extract fields with confidence and retain page/field traceability.\n3. Validate formats, arithmetic, currency, tax, supplier, duplicates, PO, receipt and approvals.\n4. Apply risk-based thresholds; separate low-confidence from high-impact conflicts.\n5. Route exceptions with source evidence and reason codes to authorised reviewers.\n6. Log corrections, overrides, approver identity and downstream outcome.\n7. Sample accepted and rejected cases to monitor false accepts, false rejects and drift.","AP validates invoice and PO evidence; Procurement/Receiving confirm commercial and receipt data; Tax checks tax treatment; Master Data controls supplier records; Security governs document access; Model owners monitor extraction quality; Audit tests traceability and override controls.","Field accuracy = correctly extracted and validated fields \xf7 fields reviewed in the sample. False-accept rate = invalid items accepted \xf7 all invalid items in the test set. Define labels and sample design before comparing results.","Create a validation checklist for invoice number, date, supplier, tax ID, currency, subtotal, tax, total, PO, receipt, duplicate risk and bank-detail change. Specify rule, evidence, failure route and owner for each.","Design a human-reviewed invoice intake workflow with confidence bands, field-level checks, exception queue, audit log, test dataset, quality metrics and safe fallback when the model or integration is unavailable.","Extraction is not approval. Validate against independent evidence and retain human accountability for risky exceptions."],t.w)
B.Of=new A.ac(B.aT,["Payment settlement investigation lab: trace a transaction across initiation, authorisation, processor status, clearing, settlement, bank statement and ledger posting. Keep pending, failed, returned, reversed and settled states distinct.","Payment operations are stateful and asynchronous. An application may show accepted while the processor is still pending; settlement may occur in a later window and fees or netting can make gross transaction totals differ from bank credits. Use stable transaction and settlement-batch identifiers, timestamps, currency and status mapping. Investigate missing, duplicate, partial and late entries before posting adjustments. Never infer final settlement from a front-end message alone.","A dashboard shows 100 payments totalling \u20b910 lakh as successful, but the settlement file contains 98 items and the bank credit is \u20b99.82 lakh. Do not post a \u20b918,000 balancing entry. Match each transaction ID, check two missing items, processing fees and settlement timing, then classify every difference with evidence and owner. Reconcile both count and value before closing the batch.","1. Capture batch ID, transaction IDs, timestamps, gross/net amount, currency and status.\n2. Map platform, processor, settlement-file and bank status definitions.\n3. Reconcile eligible transaction counts and values at each stage.\n4. Identify timing, fees, rejected items, returns, reversals and unmatched records separately.\n5. Check duplicate IDs and retries before initiating another payment.\n6. Route breaks by reason, value, age and accountable owner.\n7. Close only with evidence, authorised adjustments and documented residual exceptions.","Payments Operations owns transaction tracing; Treasury confirms bank credits and value dates; Finance reconciles ledger postings and fees; Engineering checks events and interfaces; Risk monitors suspicious patterns; Support handles customer communication under policy.","Settlement break = expected settlement amount \u2212 independently confirmed bank amount, after separately accounting for documented fees, returns, reversals and timing. Reconcile counts and IDs as well as net value.","Build a fictional reconciliation for ten transactions containing one pending item, one rejection, one duplicate risk, one fee and one late settlement. Classify each break and specify evidence and owner.","Write a payment operations runbook with state mapping, reconciliation checkpoints, exception ageing, duplicate prevention, escalation thresholds and end-of-day sign-off.","Trace each transaction across independent records; classify timing and value differences rather than forcing totals to balance."],t.w)
B.Ol=new A.ac(B.aT,["API timeout, retry and reconciliation workshop: design for uncertain outcomes. A timeout means the caller did not receive a timely response; it does not prove the provider failed to execute the request.","Reliable integrations use documented request/response contracts, authentication, least privilege, idempotency, bounded retries, timeouts, correlation IDs and monitoring. A repeated request can create duplicate financial effects unless the provider supports a valid idempotency key or the client checks status before retrying. Distinguish transport errors from business rejections. Record the original request, response or timeout, subsequent status inquiry and final reconciled state without exposing secrets in logs.","An ERP sends a \u20b925,000 payment instruction and times out before receiving a response. The safe path is to retain the transaction reference and idempotency key, query provider status using the supported mechanism, and retry only under the documented policy. Sending a fresh instruction with a new key could pay twice. If status remains unknown, route to an exception queue for controlled investigation.","1. Define API contract, authentication, permission scope and data validation.\n2. Generate a unique business transaction ID and supported idempotency key.\n3. Set bounded timeouts and retry only transient, permitted failures.\n4. Use exponential backoff or provider guidance; avoid retry storms.\n5. On timeout, query status or reconcile before creating another financial instruction.\n6. Log correlation IDs, states and outcomes while redacting credentials and sensitive data.\n7. Alert on stuck states and reconcile requests against provider and ledger records.","Product defines business semantics; Engineering implements client resilience; Security governs credentials and permissions; Payments Operations resolves unknown states; Treasury confirms settlement; Finance validates posting and reconciliation; Support communicates verified status.","Request completeness = submitted business transactions accounted for as confirmed success + confirmed rejection/hold + documented unresolved exceptions. Do not classify an unknown timeout as a success or failure without evidence.","Draw a sequence for a payment API timeout. Mark the idempotency key, status inquiry, permitted retry, audit events, exception queue and the evidence needed before the user is told the payment succeeded.","Create an integration control specification covering contract tests, auth, idempotency, retry policy, timeout recovery, observability, reconciliation, incident handling and secret-safe logs.","A timeout is an unknown state. Check status and reconcile before retrying financial operations."],t.w)
B.Ok=new A.ac(B.aT,["Root-cause analysis and process mapping lab: document how work actually flows, measure waiting and rework, validate suspected causes with evidence and avoid jumping directly to automation.","A process map makes activities, decisions, queues, handoffs, systems, evidence and control points visible. Root-cause tools such as the 5 Whys and fishbone diagrams generate hypotheses, not proof. Validate with transaction samples, time stamps, exception codes and staff interviews. Separate symptoms (late invoices) from causes (missing receipt, unclear ownership, invalid master data or delayed interface). Check whether the proposed fix moves work or risk to another team.","A team believes late supplier payments are caused by slow AP processing. A sample of 100 overdue invoices shows 42 awaiting receipt confirmation, 25 blocked by price differences, 18 awaiting approval and 15 with AP processing delay. The evidence suggests most delay is upstream. A generic AP automation would not solve the largest causes; Receiving and Procurement need measurable resolution actions.","1. Define the problem, scope, customer impact and dated baseline.\n2. Observe representative cases from start to finish, including exceptions.\n3. Map activities, queues, handoffs, systems, controls and decision points.\n4. Quantify volume, elapsed time, touch time, rework and failure reasons.\n5. Use root-cause tools to form hypotheses and test them against records.\n6. Rank causes by evidence, impact and controllability.\n7. Select a pilot that addresses a validated cause and measures side effects.","Process owners explain operating rules; frontline staff validate real work; Data analysts quantify patterns; IT confirms system events; Risk/Audit checks control points; managers assign cross-team ownership and remove blockers.","Lead time = completion timestamp \u2212 start timestamp. Touch time excludes waiting time. Rework rate = cases requiring repeat processing \xf7 total cases, with the case grain and period defined.","Map a fictional invoice from receipt to payment. Identify at least six steps, three handoffs, two controls, one queue and three suspected causes; state the evidence needed to validate each cause.","Submit a current-state process map, baseline metrics, root-cause evidence table, prioritised improvement options and a pilot measurement plan with guardrails.","Observe the real process, measure queues and rework, validate root causes and improve the cause before automating."],t.w)
B.Om=new A.ac(B.aT,["Acceptance criteria and benefits tracking workshop: translate a business problem into testable delivery conditions and a measurable outcome plan. \u201cBuilt\u201d and \u201creleased\u201d are not proof that the intended benefit occurred.","A strong user story identifies the role, need and business value. Acceptance criteria define observable pass/fail behaviour, including normal cases, edge cases, permissions, reconciliation and failure handling. A Definition of Done covers quality and release readiness. Benefits tracking separately defines baseline, target, metric owner, calculation, data source, timing, assumptions and control guardrails. Avoid claiming savings from time released unless a credible redeployment or cost reduction is evidenced.","Story: \u201cAs an AP team lead, I want blocked invoices grouped by root cause and owner so that I can reduce overdue exposure.\u201d Criteria include every in-scope invoice appearing once, totals reconciling to the AP source, unknown reasons remaining visible, role-based access, refresh timestamp, and a failed-refresh alert. The benefit target might be a 20% reduction in median resolution time after eight weeks, without increasing duplicate payments or control overrides.","1. State the user, problem, business value and current baseline.\n2. Write criteria that are measurable, observable and independently testable.\n3. Include access, data completeness, exception behaviour, reconciliation and audit evidence.\n4. Define test data for normal, boundary, missing and failure cases.\n5. Set Definition of Done, owners, dependencies and release checks.\n6. Define outcome metrics, source, target, review period and guardrails.\n7. Review actual post-launch results and document whether benefits were achieved.","Business owner defines value; Product turns it into a backlog item; Engineering implements and documents behaviour; QA tests edge cases; Data validates measures; Risk/Audit checks control evidence; Operations confirms adoption and reports outcomes.","Benefit achievement % = validated benefit achieved \xf7 approved target benefit \xd7 100%, only when the measures and periods are comparable. Improvement % = (baseline \u2212 new result) \xf7 baseline for lower-is-better metrics.","Write one user story and at least seven pass/fail criteria for an AP exception dashboard. Include source reconciliation, missing data, access, refresh failure, exception visibility and a benefit guardrail.","Prepare a delivery-ready backlog item with story, acceptance tests, Definition of Done, owner/dependencies, rollout plan, baseline/target, benefit evidence and a 30/60/90-day review schedule.","Acceptance criteria prove the feature works; baseline and post-launch evidence prove whether it created business value."],t.w)
B.Or=new A.ac(B.aT,["Workbook design and source-data discipline for finance: build a clean structure before adding formulas.","A maintainable workbook separates five layers: raw input, cleaned data, calculations, validation checks and presentation. Keep the original source unchanged; use Excel Tables so formulas and references expand as rows are added. Use one row per transaction, one field per column, unique headers, consistent data types and explicit definitions for status, currency and reporting date. Avoid merged cells in source data and avoid embedding assumptions inside long formulas.","A supplier file has 500 rows, but a pasted total is \u20b948.2 lakh while the source control total is \u20b949.1 lakh. Before building charts, check filters, hidden rows, duplicate invoice IDs, text-formatted amounts, credits and missing rows. A dashboard cannot repair an incomplete source.","1. Save an untouched source copy and record its received date.\n2. Convert the data range to a named Table.\n3. Confirm one transaction per row and one attribute per column.\n4. Standardise date, amount, currency and status types.\n5. Add checks for row count, duplicate keys, blanks and source totals.\n6. Keep assumptions and report date in clearly labelled cells.\n7. Add a short instructions and change-log section.","AP validates transaction meaning; GL/R2R confirms accounting totals; Treasury validates payment status; data owners explain field definitions; Audit checks evidence, access and reproducibility.","Control difference = source control total \u2212 workbook total. Expected result is zero within an explicitly approved tolerance; also reconcile record counts and investigate whether both sources share the same omission.","Create a workbook layout with tabs named Read Me, Raw Data, Clean Data, Checks, Analysis and Dashboard. Define the purpose, owner and key control for each tab.","Submit a starter finance workbook with a data dictionary, source evidence, validation checks and a documented refresh procedure.","Structure and reconcile the data before analysis. A polished workbook is not reliable unless its lineage and checks are visible."],t.w)
B.Oj=new A.ac(B.aT,["Core formulas and logic: calculate repeatable results while exposing missing or invalid data.","Relative references move when copied; absolute references lock a row and column; mixed references lock only one dimension. SUMIFS and COUNTIFS support criteria-based analysis. IF with AND/OR encodes business rules, while date functions calculate due dates and ageing. IFERROR should handle a known, expected error path\u2014not blanket-hide broken keys or invalid data.","For an unpaid invoice, overdue days may be MAX(0, report date \u2212 due date). If due date is blank or the invoice is paid, the output should not falsely show overdue days. Make the rule explicit and test all branches.","1. Write the business rule in plain language.\n2. Identify required fields and valid values.\n3. Choose the simplest formula that expresses the rule.\n4. Use named assumptions or absolute references for fixed cells.\n5. Test normal, blank, invalid, duplicate and boundary cases.\n6. Compare summary totals to an independent control total.\n7. Document formula meaning and known limitations.","Finance defines the rule; AP validates payment status; Treasury confirms report-date needs; Data/IT supports field types; a reviewer tests exceptions.",'Example: =IF(AND([@Status]="Unpaid",ISNUMBER([@[Due Date]])),MAX(0,$B$2-[@[Due Date]]),""). Adapt the column names and business rules to the actual table.',"Write formulas for overdue days, overdue flag, duplicate invoice identifier and amount variance. Add a test matrix for paid, unpaid, blank due date, future due date and invalid date.","Deliver a formula library with plain-language rule, formula, sample input/output and exception handling for at least eight finance checks.","Make the rule explicit, test edge cases and never hide data problems just to make a formula return a clean-looking result."],t.w)
B.Oq=new A.ac(B.aT,["Lookups and dynamic arrays: join datasets carefully and make missing or duplicate matches visible.","XLOOKUP retrieves a value based on a key; INDEX/MATCH offers a widely compatible alternative. Exact-match behaviour should be deliberate. A lookup is only as reliable as its keys: duplicate supplier IDs, inconsistent formatting and missing IDs can create incorrect or ambiguous results. Dynamic arrays such as FILTER, SORT and UNIQUE can create responsive analysis lists in supported Excel versions; confirm version compatibility before distributing the workbook.","An invoice contains supplier code SUP-014, but the supplier master has two rows for that code with different payment terms. A lookup returning the first row can conceal a master-data defect. Test key uniqueness and route duplicates to the master-data owner.","1. Identify the business key and confirm its uniqueness in the lookup table.\n2. Standardise whitespace, case and data types where appropriate.\n3. Use exact-match logic and an explicit not-found result.\n4. Create separate checks for missing and duplicate keys.\n5. Reconcile match rate and review exceptions.\n6. Test behaviour with duplicate, blank and unknown keys.\n7. Document supported Excel version and refresh expectations.","AP provides transaction context; Procurement owns supplier details; Master Data resolves duplicate identities; IT supports source integration; Audit reviews changes to sensitive master fields.",'Example: =XLOOKUP([@SupplierID],SupplierMaster[SupplierID],SupplierMaster[PaymentTerms],"REVIEW",0). A separate duplicate-key check is required; the lookup alone is insufficient.',"Join a fictional invoice table to a supplier master. Produce lists for matched, missing and duplicate supplier IDs and explain how each exception should be resolved.","Build a supplier-validation report that prevents ambiguous or missing master-data matches from silently flowing into payment analysis.","Use exact matches, test key uniqueness and show exceptions. A successful lookup is not proof of a correct match."],t.w)
B.Os=new A.ac(B.aT,["Power Query: create a repeatable, auditable monthly file-cleaning and reconciliation pipeline.","Power Query records data preparation steps such as import, type conversion, trimming, splitting, merging, appending, grouping and filtering. Merge joins tables using keys; append combines rows from compatible structures. A refresh can succeed technically while returning incomplete or misclassified data, so use explicit validation checks and a visible exception path.","Two AP files have different column order, supplier names with trailing spaces and amounts stored as text. Import both, standardise column names and types, trim text, append rows, flag missing invoice IDs and reconcile the output row count and amount total to the combined inputs.","1. Preserve and identify each source file.\n2. Import using a repeatable folder or file pattern.\n3. Rename columns and set data types explicitly.\n4. Trim/clean text and standardise dates and amounts.\n5. Merge or append using documented keys and rules.\n6. Output rejected rows and schema exceptions separately.\n7. Reconcile row counts and monetary totals after every refresh.\n8. Test a new month containing missing and extra columns before go-live.","AP validates business fields; Data/IT maintains source structure; GL/R2R validates totals; process owners resolve rejected rows; Audit reviews transformation logic and evidence.","Input rows \u2212 excluded rows = output rows, when the documented rules imply no other row-changing operation. Input control total \u2212 output control total must be explained, not simply ignored.","Combine two fictional monthly files, clean supplier names, standardise data types and create a separate rejected-records query. Record row counts and amount totals before and after.","Deliver a refreshable query pipeline with source instructions, transformation steps, an exception output and a control-total checklist.","Repeatable transformation saves time; reconciliation proves whether the refreshed result remains complete and correct."],t.w)
B.Oc=new A.ac(B.aT,["Capstone: create a finance report that is reconciled, decision-ready and governed.","A dashboard should answer a defined decision question and trace back to validated data. Define each KPI\u2019s numerator, denominator, population, report date, exclusions, refresh frequency and owner. Include a clear exception path and distinguish measured facts from estimates. Use protection and versioning as practical controls, not as substitutes for access governance or review.","An AP pack reports \u20b98 lakh overdue. The reviewer should be able to trace the figure to the source extract, see whether disputed and partially paid invoices are included, confirm the report date, inspect ageing bucket logic and identify owners for the largest exceptions.","1. Confirm scope, source and report date.\n2. Refresh the query and validate row counts and control totals.\n3. Check duplicate, missing and invalid records.\n4. Calculate defined KPIs and ageing buckets.\n5. Show trends, material exceptions and action owners.\n6. Document assumptions, exclusions and refresh instructions.\n7. Obtain reviewer sign-off and retain the output with source evidence.","AP owns invoice status; Treasury uses payment timing; Procurement resolves PO blocks; GL/R2R validates liability totals; management prioritises actions; Audit/Risk reviews controls and traceability.","Overdue rate = overdue unpaid invoice amount \xf7 total unpaid invoice amount \xd7 100%, if amount-based rate is the agreed definition. Do not mix count-based and amount-based denominators.","Create a dashboard with open balance, overdue balance, ageing buckets, top five exceptions, missing-data count and reconciliation status. Write definitions for every KPI.","Present a monthly AP risk pack, including source data, workbook/query logic, validation checks, dashboard, exception log, KPI dictionary and reviewer checklist.","A strong finance workbook is accurate, traceable, refreshable, well-defined and tied to accountable actions."],t.w)
